/*
Problem: Check if Array is Good (LC 2784)
Topic: Hashing / Frequency Count

--------------------------------------------------

Idea:

A good array of size n must contain:

1, 2, 3, ..., n-1 exactly once

and

n-1 exactly twice.

So simply count the frequency of
every number and verify these
conditions.

--------------------------------------------------

Algorithm:

1. Store the frequency of every
   element using a hash map.

2. Check numbers from 1 to n-2:
   Each must appear exactly once.

3. Check n-1:
   It must appear exactly twice.

4. If all conditions hold,
   return true.
   Otherwise return false.

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

[1,2,3,3]

Size = 4

Expected:

1 -> 1 time

2 -> 1 time

3 -> 2 times

Valid

Answer:

true

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

[1,2,2]

Size = 3

Expected:

1 -> 1 time

2 -> 2 times

Valid

Answer:

true

--------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

A good array has a fixed frequency
pattern.

Checking frequencies directly is
enough to determine whether the
array satisfies the definition.

--------------------------------------------------

Time : O(n)

One pass to build frequencies and
one pass to verify.

Space : O(n)

Hash map stores frequencies.

--------------------------------------------------

Pattern:

Hashing + Frequency Counting
*/

class Solution {
public:
    bool isGood(vector<int>& nums) {

        unordered_map<int, int> mp;

        // Count frequency
        for (int x : nums) {
            mp[x]++;
        }

        // 1 to n-2 should appear once
        for (int i = 1; i <= nums.size() - 2; i++) {
            if (mp[i] != 1)
                return false;
        }

        // n-1 should appear twice
        if (mp[nums.size() - 1] != 2)
            return false;

        return true;
    }
};
